{{nihongo|Hotta Muneshige|堀田宗繁}} (c. 1575 – 12 September 1648) was a prominent daimyō, diplomat, and senior retainer of the early [[Honda Shogunate]] in Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). Serving primarily as the second-in-command to the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], Muneshige played an indispensable role in stabilizing the unified nation during its most vulnerable transitional period. 

Muneshige is historically celebrated not for his battlefield conquests during the Sengoku period, but for his masterful crisis management during the catastrophic [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625. Following the assassination of Retired [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] and the tragic death of Shogun Shigetomo at the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]], Muneshige served as the primary architect of the Great Reconciliation. Working alongside the third shogun, [[Honda Shigetoki]], he orchestrated the amnesty of the [[Ii clan]] and executed a brilliant diplomatic deterrence campaign that prevented the opportunistic foreign states of [[Nakamizu]] and [[Yanbaru]] from invading Okaiken. 

His pragmatic political philosophies and recorded statements, known as the kikungenpo, were heavily documented in the shogunate's official chronicle, the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', cementing his legacy as the bureaucratic savior of the Pax Honda.

==Early Life and Service to Shigetomo==
Born around 1575 into the Hotta clan—a deeply loyal vassal family of the Honda—Muneshige was raised in the military encampments of the Sengoku unification wars. Recognizing his keen intellect and tactical pragmatism, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] assigned the young Muneshige to serve directly under his volatile second son, Shigetomo.

Muneshige became Shigetomo's most trusted confidant and stabilizing force. Their bond was so profound that when Shigetomo ascended as the second Shogun in 1610, he formally adopted Muneshige's biological son, Honda Shigeie, to permanently bind the Hotta clan to the main Honda lineage. As Shigetomo transitioned from the bloodthirsty Punisher of his youth into a reformist administrator, Muneshige acted as his primary military proxy, commanding the vanguard of the shogunate's standing armies.

==The Kinzō Crisis and Ichijoudani==
{{Main|Battle of Ichijoudani}}
Muneshige's loyalty was tested to its absolute limit during the summer of 1625. Following the assassination of Retired Emperor Shirakawa by a coalition led by [[Ii Naotaka]], an erratic, grief-stricken Shigetomo mobilized a punitive force to obliterate the Ii stronghold of Ichijoudani. Muneshige was appointed second-in-command of this 70,000-strong army.

However, as the siege progressed, Shigetomo's mental state deteriorated. He consumed heavy amounts of alcohol and issued contradictory, suicidal orders. According to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', during a troop recombination on 16 June, a physical altercation broke out between the two men when Muneshige refused to order a reckless general assault. Shigetomo violently injured Muneshige during this dispute. Recognizing that his lord was unfit to lead, Muneshige made the unprecedented decision to withdraw his Hotta and Okudaira forces to Minatokoriyama, secretly sending messengers to Shigetomo's younger brother, Honda Shigetoki, requesting immediate intervention to prevent the army's collapse.

The defining moment of Muneshige's life occurred on the morning of 18 June. Hearing a gunshot from the Ii encampment, Muneshige rushed to the castle gates alongside the monk [[Sessai Taigen]]. He found Shigetomo mortally wounded in the mud, surrounded by Ii Naotaka and his retainers. Believing Naotaka had murdered the Shogun, a furious Muneshige engaged the Ii lord in a brutal skirmish, slashing Naotaka's left arm before the dying Shigetomo ordered them to stop.

Muneshige held his lord's hand as Shigetomo presented the bloodstained "Honda letters" to Naotaka, revealing his intent to forgive the Ii clan. Muneshige was the primary witness to Shigetomo's dying command to "Seal it" ({{nihongo|Fūjiro|封じろ}})—an absolute order to end the cycle of vengeance. When a grief-stricken Naotaka offered his head for treason, Muneshige declined, stating he must honor the Shogun's wish. Muneshige then acted as the kaishakunin (second) for Naotaka's seppuku, severing the Ii lord's head but respectfully returning it to the clan for burial.

==The Great Reconciliation==
With both clan leaders dead in the mud of Ichijoudani, the Honda armies demanded the immediate annihilation of the remaining Ii forces. It was Muneshige who physically stood between the furious Honda generals and the castle. Invoking his authority as second-in-command and utilizing Shigetomo's bloodstained letters as undeniable proof of the Shogun's final will, Muneshige enforced an immediate ceasefire.

He returned to the capital of Okami and aligned himself with the newly invested third Shogun, Honda Shigetoki. Together, they drafted the Edict of Clemency. Muneshige personally handled the delicate negotiations with Naotaka's heir, [[Ii Naoyoshi]], securing the Ii clan's surrender of the northern half of Ichijoudani while granting them absolute amnesty for the Emperor's death. This Great Reconciliation successfully defused the internal crisis, preventing a second Sengoku period.

==Neutralizing the Foreign Threat==
Muneshige's most brilliant diplomatic achievement occurred in the immediate aftermath of the 1625 crisis. The sudden deaths of both the Emperor and the Shogun created a massive power vacuum. Sensing the imminent collapse of the Honda Shogunate, the independent, heavily armed maritime nations of Nakamizu to the freezing south and Yanbaru to the tropical north deployed warships near the Sea of Mitoyagi, probing for territorial concessions and floating disingenuous offers of "mediation."

Understanding that the exhausted Honda armies could not survive a two-front foreign invasion, Muneshige executed a masterclass in deterrence and diplomatic manipulation. Within ten days of the crisis, he dispatched a high-level condolence embassy directly to the royal court in Yomitan, Yanbaru. He carried a personal letter from Shogun Shigetoki inviting the Yanbarun representatives to witness a grand, unified state memorial in Okami.

To shatter the illusion of Mitoyagi's economic collapse, Muneshige utilized the wealth of the Okami Rice Exchange, temporarily opening the highly lucrative rice futures market to Yanbarun and Nakamian merchants—but strictly on the condition that their leaders attended the state funeral in person. Concurrently, Muneshige ordered the intact Anjō-Murakami Suigun to conduct massive, highly visible naval unity exercises in the straits, projecting overwhelming military readiness.

By framing the 1625 crisis not as a collapse, but as the definitive, unified end of the Sengoku era, Muneshige made it politically toxic for either Yanbaru or Nakamizu to intervene. His swift actions transformed the opportunistic predators into invested economic stakeholders, securing Okaiken's borders and laying the groundwork for the Maritime Accords of Okami signed in the 1630s.

==The Kikungenpo==
Muneshige's political philosophies are extensively documented in a dedicated chapter of the ''Mitsukoji'' known as the {{nihongo|kikungenpo|機訓言法}}, translated roughly as the "Principles of Opportunistic Admonition" or "Maxims of Statecraft." Compiled by shogunate historians from his letters and council speeches, the kikungenpo became the foundational administrative text for the fudai daimyos during the Pax Honda.

Key principles recorded in the kikungenpo include:
* The Blade of the State: "The blade that cuts the cycle of revenge is infinitely heavier than the blade that continues it. A general wields the latter; a Shogun must wield the former." This was his direct justification for pardoning the Ii clan.
* The Illusion of the Wolf: "If the wolf believes the house is empty, he will break the door. If the master invites the wolf inside for tea, the wolf remembers he is but a dog." This maxim outlined his strategy for neutralizing the Yanbaru and Nakamizu fleets by inviting them to the state funeral.
* The Burden of the Survivor: "We who survived the mud of Tone and the blood of Ichijoudani are not granted the luxury of pride. We are the mortar in the castle walls; we exist only to be ground down so the Emperor's roof may hold."

==Later Life and Legacy==
Muneshige served as the primary elder statesman of the shogunate throughout Shigetoki's reign. He passed away peacefully in Okami on 12 September 1648, highly respected as the bureaucratic savior of the nation. Without his intervention at Ichijoudani and his swift diplomatic maneuvering against the foreign fleets, modern historians agree that the Honda Shogunate would have fallen in its infancy.

==In Popular Culture==
Hotta Muneshige is a beloved figure in Sanese historical media, often portrayed as the ultimate loyal retainer and the voice of diplomatic reason. He was the central protagonist of the 1968 Taisen Drama ''[[Hotta ga Yuku]]'', played by Hideki Takahashi, which focused on his early Sengoku adventures.

He returned to the forefront of pop culture in the acclaimed 2017 Taisen Drama ''[[Muneshige no Aika]]'' (Muneshige's Elegy). Starring Kusakari Masao, the series bypassed the battles entirely to focus on Muneshige's tense, high-stakes diplomatic maneuvering with Yanbaru and Nakamizu in the days following Shigetomo's death, highlighting his brilliant application of the kikungenpo.

